  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Amplified upper-level pattern continues \nthis afternoon across NOAM. Longwave troughing remains evident over \nthe eastern half of the continent, including the Great Lakes, with \nupstream ridging over the southern Plains/desert southwest northward \nthrough the Rockies/western Plains. Sfc low pressure sits off the \nNortheast coast with broad cyclonic flow locally aiding to continue \nto draw in cold air from the north. During the day Saturday, heights \ngradually begin to rise as upstream ridging makes slow headway \ntowards the Great Lakes. Attendant sprawling sfc high pressure will \nfollow suit with both of these features aiding to bring a return of \nquieter (and eventually warmer) weather Sunday through at least the \nmiddle of next week. \n\nForecast Details: Primary focus continues to revolve around ongoing \nlake effect snow this afternoon through tonight, with potential for \nlocalized snow accumulation and associated impacts to continue. \nWinds still out of the north-northeast early this afternoon, but \nsubtly turn more out of the due north later this afternoon and \neventually more north-northwest later tonight into Saturday. Given \nthe above, ongoing rain/snow showers most numerous over north-\nnortheast flow lake belts of far northwest lower MI (GT Bay + points \nwest/southwest) and over far northeast lower near the Lake Huron \ncoast. This should eventually transition to more traditional north \nflow areas as the day wears on, but really isn't all that big of a \nchange from present. Embedded shortwave this evening may flare \nup additional, more numerous, showers inland as well for a time \nbefore that limited synoptic support exits south overnight. \nPrimary precip type likely to remain snow inland, with more rain\nmixing in at the immediate lakeshore. \n\nIn terms of accumulation this afternoon through tonight, primary \narea of focus continues to be around the Grand Traverse Bay region, \ninitially Leelanau County, eventually transitioning to downwind of \nthe bay across parts of central Grand Traverse and perhaps into \nnorthern Wexford counties. Latest snow accum. probabilities across \ncentral Grand Traverse County for late this afternoon through \nSaturday morning include 50-70% chances of 2\"+, and 15-25% chance of \n4\"+. Spotty sub-5% for 6\"+. Outside of this area, probabilities for \nmeaningful snow are much lower, with generally an inch or less of \nnew snow expected across the remainder of northwest lower, and some \nspotty minor accumulation possible across the remainder of north-\ncentral/northeast lower into parts of the eastern U.P.  In terms of \nheadlines, while deterministic accumulation won't necessarily \nachieve winter weather advisory criteria, will opt to extend \nLeelanau County for a few more hours and for Grand Traverse County \nthrough 12z Saturday, with the scales being tipped in that direction \ngiven this is still the first snow event of the season. \n\nDuring the day Saturday, lingering lake induced precip likely to be \nmixed with or turn to more rain, and really may wind up more drizzly \nas the morning wears on and moisture depth really struggles. Warm \nadvection gradually ramps up through the day, albeit slowly, with a \nlimited lake response remaining possible perhaps all the way \nthrough Saturday night. \n\nHigh pressure settles overhead Sunday with quiet weather anticipated \nSunday through at least the middle of next week. Big story for next \nweek will be a return to well above normal temperatures as latest \ntrends supporting high temps in the 40s on Sunday before rising \nto the low-mid 50s Monday through at least Thursday. Mid-\nNovember normal highs range from 40-45 F across the forecast \narea.",
